          "name": "mAh vs Wh, which spec should OEM buyers use for power bank procurement?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "mAh (milliamp-hours) measures charge capacity at a specific voltage (typically 3.7V for lithium cells). Wh (watt-hours) measures total energy, it's voltage-independent and the regulatory standard. Conversion: Wh = (mAh × 3.7V) ÷ 1,000. Example: 27,000mAh × 3.7V ÷ 1,000 = 99.9Wh. Always compare power banks using Wh for procurement decisions. Airline compliance threshold: ≤100Wh (~27,000mAh) allowed in carry-on without approval; 100-160Wh requires airline approval (2-unit max); >160Wh prohibited. Amazon FBA requires Wh on product detail pages. WOWOHCOOL prints both mAh and Wh on all OEM power banks as standard."
          }
