 <h2 class="text-lg font-black uppercase text-amber-700 mb-3">Key Takeaways</h2>
 <ul class="text-sm text-slate-700 space-y-2 list-disc pl-5">
 <li><strong>Cell capacity vs rated capacity: the 35% gap every OEM buyer must account for</strong>. A "10,000mAh" power bank uses a 3.7V lithium cell rated at 10,000mAh, but USB output at 5V delivers ~6,000-6,500mAh after voltage conversion loss. Never print cell capacity on retail packaging, print the rated USB output capacity to avoid customer complaints and regulatory issues.</li>
 <li><strong>Li-Polymer vs Li-ion (18650): choose based on product positioning</strong>. Li-Polymer: thinner (can achieve <10mm enclosure), 10-20% higher cost, 500-cycle lifespan, premium look. 18650 Li-ion: cylindrical, thicker (>18mm), lower cost, 300-500 cycles, easier to replace cells. Premium brands use Li-Po; budget/value brands use 18650.</li>
 <li><strong>PD 3.1 with PPS is the minimum viable protocol for 2026 OEM power banks</strong>. QC 3.0 is legacy (Qualcomm proprietary, fading). PD 3.1 + PPS covers iPhone, Samsung, Google Pixel, and most laptops. A power bank without PPS will deliver standard 5V/2A to Samsung devices regardless of wattage rating, a top-3 customer complaint category.</li>
 <li><strong>4 certifications every OEM power bank must have before shipping</strong>: CE (EU), FCC (US), UN38.3 (lithium battery air transport), and RoHS. For the EU market, add WEEE registration and EU Battery Regulation 2023/1542 compliance. Budget $2,000-4,000 for the full certification package, 4-6 weeks lead time.</li>
 </ul>
