 <!-- FAQ -->
 <section id="faq" class="mb-16">
 <div class="bg-slate-50 rounded-2xl p-8 border border-slate-200 shadow-sm">
 <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-brandBlue uppercase italic mb-8 text-center">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
 <div class="space-y-6 max-w-3xl mx-auto">
 <div class="bg-white rounded-xl p-6 faq-answer">
 <h3 class="font-black text-brandBlue mb-2">mAh vs Wh, which spec should OEM buyers use for power bank procurement?</h3>
 <p class="text-slate-600 text-sm"><strong>mAh</strong> measures charge at a specific voltage (3.7V for lithium cells). <strong>Wh</strong> measures total energy, it's voltage-independent and the regulatory standard. Conversion: Wh = (mAh × 3.7V) ÷ 1,000. Always compare power banks using Wh. <strong>Critical for OEM buyers: the 35% capacity gap</strong>, a 10,000mAh cell delivers only ~6,000-6,500mAh at 5V USB output due to voltage conversion loss. Print rated USB output capacity on packaging, not cell capacity. Airline limit: <strong>≤100Wh (~27,000mAh)</strong> in carry-on without approval. Amazon FBA requires Wh on product pages. WOWOHCOOL prints both mAh and Wh on all OEM power banks.</p>
 </div>
 <div class="bg-white rounded-xl p-6 faq-answer">
 <h3 class="font-black text-brandBlue mb-2">What wattage power bank do I need for laptop charging, 65W, 100W, or 140W?</h3>
 <p class="text-slate-600 text-sm"><strong>MacBook Air: 30-45W. MacBook Pro 13": 45-67W. MacBook Pro 16": 67-140W</strong>, requires PD 3.1 EPR. Dell XPS/ThinkPad: 45-65W. A 65W PD 3.0 bank covers ~85% of ultrabooks; 140W PD 3.1 EPR covers all laptops. EU laptop USB-C mandate (April 2026) makes PD power banks a growth category. Multi-port with <strong>dynamic power allocation</strong> charges laptop + phone simultaneously, verify the split ratio (65W+20W vs fixed 45W+45W).</p>
 </div>
 <div class="bg-white rounded-xl p-6 faq-answer">
 <h3 class="font-black text-brandBlue mb-2">Maximum power bank mAh for flights — IATA limits and OEM design rules?</h3>
 <p class="text-slate-600 text-sm"><strong>≤100Wh (~27,000mAh):</strong> carry-on, no approval, practical product ceiling. <strong>100-160Wh:</strong> airline approval, max 2 units. <strong>>160Wh:</strong> prohibited. Critical for OEM: 27,000mAh = 99.9Wh, exactly at the limit. Products above 27,000mAh become restricted. Air freight: Class 9 Dangerous Goods (UN3480), UN38.3 required, ≤30% charge state. WOWOHCOOL handles all DG declarations.</p>
 </div>
 
 
 <div class="bg-white rounded-xl p-6 faq-answer">
 <h3 class="font-black text-brandBlue mb-2">GB47372-2026, how does China's new power bank safety standard affect OEM buyers?</h3>
 <p class="text-slate-600 text-sm">Published March 2026, enforceable <strong>April 2027</strong>. Tightens: Grade-A cell minimum, protection circuits, enhanced UN38.3 testing, CCC with traceable QR codes. <strong>~70% of low-end manufacturers expected to exit.</strong> For OEM buyers: verify factory GB47372-readiness NOW, non-compliant factories face shutdown. WOWOHCOOL is already GB47372-compliant, ensuring supply chain continuity.</p>
 </div>
 
 <div class="bg-white rounded-xl p-6 faq-answer">
 <h3 class="font-black text-brandBlue mb-2">How do I source OEM power banks with the right specs for my target market?</h3>
 <p class="text-slate-600 text-sm"><strong>Step 1:</strong> Capacity, 10,000mAh for volume (47.89% share, FOB $4-7), 20,000mAh for premium, 27,000mAh for max airline-compliant. <strong>Step 2:</strong> Battery tech, Li-polymer for budget-to-mid, semi-solid-state for premium. <strong>Step 3:</strong> PD wattage, 30W phones, 65W ultrabooks, 140W PD 3.1 laptops. <strong>Step 4:</strong> Certifications, CE/FCC/UL/UN38.3 + GB47372-2026 ready. <strong>Step 5:</strong> Demand Grade-A cell cert + 0.2C discharge reports. MOQ 500, 25-30 day lead. <strong>WOWOHCOOL: 29+ OEM models, 5,000-27,000mAh, Li-polymer + semi-solid-state, full certs. <a href="/contact/" class="text-brandBlue hover:text-brandOrange underline">Request power bank OEM consultation.</a></strong></p>
 </div>
 </div>
 </div>
